# fleet-kit configuration. Copy to fleet.env and fill in. Every script in this kit sources
# this file (or reads the same-named env vars) instead of hardcoding a host, path, or label.
#
# Provenance: genericized from nonprofit-atlas's ~/.config/990scout/env pattern
# (docs/ops/deploy.md) — the source fleet hardcoded these as `atlas-serve`, `lucky2`,
# `990scout`, `/opt/philanthropy`. Every one of those strings becomes a variable here.

# --- kill switches --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FLEET_ENABLED gates the WHOLE fleet -- every member script exits immediately, before touching
# the board or spending anything, when this is false. FLEET_<MEMBER>_ENABLED gates one member
# at a time on top of that (both must be true for a member to run). Deploy with everything
# false, then flip members on one at a time and watch each in the wild before enabling the
# next -- that's what these are FOR, not just an off switch for an emergency.
# Both are read fresh on every invocation (no restart needed): edit this file, or use the
# toggle on fleet_view.html (same mechanism, same file, underneath).
FLEET_ENABLED=false
FLEET_BUILDER_ENABLED=false
FLEET_REVIEWER_ENABLED=false

# FLEET_CRON_MEMBERS (gh#138): optional allowlist of member names entrypoint.sh's
# container-native crontab should actually schedule. Space or comma separated (e.g.
# "judge-judy" or "judge-judy,jefe"). Leave unset for today's default: every member
# entrypoint.sh knows about goes on cron, same as before this existed. A misspelled or
# unknown name fails the container's boot loudly rather than silently scheduling nothing.
# Only affects the container-native scheduler (entrypoint.sh); it does not gate
# worktree_builder.sh/judge-judy.sh, which already consult FLEET_BUILDER_ENABLED/
# FLEET_REVIEWER_ENABLED above.
#FLEET_CRON_MEMBERS=

# --- identity -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Absolute path to the git repo this fleet builds against. Every script cd's here first.
FLEET_REPO=/path/to/your/repo

# org/repo slug for fleet-kit's OWN GitHub repo, used by self_improve_score.sh to also count
# self-evolution PRs merged into fleet-kit itself. Only needed when the fleet-kit checkout the
# scripts run from (KIT_DIR) has no .git (e.g. a vendored/deployed copy like /fleet-kit) --
# a live git checkout derives this from `git remote` automatically and this can stay unset.
#KIT_REPO_SLUG=your-org/fleet-kit

# Prefix for GitHub labels the board uses (fleet:backlog, fleet:claimed, {prefix}lane:<x>).
# Change this if you run multiple fleet-kit instances against the same repo (rare) or want
# labels namespaced alongside existing project labels.
FLEET_LABEL_PREFIX=fleet:

# --- Claude accounts ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Space-separated account identifiers, tried in this order by account_pool.sh. Each name
# must have a corresponding `claude` CLI auth profile (see that script's header for the
# exact lookup it does) and, optionally, a maxx-style budget-verdict endpoint if you use one.
# A single-account setup just needs one name here — the failover logic still works, it just
# never has anywhere to fail over TO.
FLEET_ACCOUNTS="primary"

# Host directory holding each account's credentials, mounted into the container as
# /root/.claude-<account>. Defaults to the deploying user's $HOME, which is right on almost
# every box; set it only if you keep credentials somewhere else. (Was hardcoded to
# /home/ubuntu until 2026-08-26 -- on any other host the mounts were created under a path
# nothing read, and every account came up logged out after each deploy.)
#FLEET_CREDS_DIR="$HOME"

# --- Lane-analysis credentials (nerd) ---------------------------------------------------------
# Each analysis lane reads its KPI from somewhere, and some of those live behind a third-party
# login. A nerd whose credential is absent cannot measure its lane -- its charter tells it to
# FILE that as a finding naming the variable, never to invent a number, but the finding recurs
# every pass until the credential lands here. Leave any of these unset if you do not use that
# source; the nerd will say so rather than guess.
#
# run_member.sh sources this file before a pass starts, so anything set here is in the pass's
# environment by name. Values are never echoed by a member -- the charters name variables only.
#
# growth: Google Search Console -- indexation coverage, query performance.
#   NOTE there is no bulk API for the aggregate "how many pages are indexed" number; that one
#   is read off the GSC UI by a human. These cover the per-URL and query APIs that DO exist.
#   These are SERVICE-ACCOUNT credentials: *_SA_KEY holds a PATH to a JSON key file, not the
#   key itself, so the file must exist and be readable by the pass. Verified live 2026-08-26.
#   Point FLEET_ANALYTICS_CREDS_DIR at the HOST directory holding the key file; deploy.sh
#   mounts it read-only at /fleet-kit/.analytics, so *_SA_KEY below is a path INSIDE the
#   container. Unset it and the mount is skipped -- an instance that does not analyse these
#   lanes is unaffected, and a nerd that needs it files the gap by name rather than guessing.
#FLEET_ANALYTICS_CREDS_DIR=/root/.config/atlas
#GSC_SA_KEY=/fleet-kit/.analytics/service-account.json
#GSC_PROPERTY=sc-domain:example.org
# growth + datadog: GA4 sessions/funnels. GA4_SA_KEY is a service-account JSON blob or a path.
#GA4_PROPERTY_ID=
#GA4_SA_KEY=
# datadog + ui: Microsoft Clarity -- session replay, rage clicks, dead clicks.
#CLARITY_API_TOKEN=
# datadog: PostHog -- product events and funnels (read side; the app already ships a write key).
#POSTHOG_PROJECT_ID=
#POSTHOG_PERSONAL_API_KEY=

# Model tier for the code reviewer. Bump to a stronger model for security/architecture-heavy
# repos; the default is calibrated for ordinary feature-diff review.
FLEET_CODE_REVIEW_MODEL=sonnet
FLEET_CODE_REVIEW_TIMEOUT=900

# Space-separated GitHub status-check names that must be green before the reviewer will
# approve/merge a PR (your repo's real CI job names — e.g. "test lint"). Empty means the
# reviewer doesn't gate on any specific check by name (still reads overall mergeable state).
# Get this wrong (a check name that never runs) and every PR stalls forever waiting on it --
# verify the exact names via `gh pr checks <any-open-pr>` on your repo first.
FLEET_REQUIRED_CHECKS=

# Hard per-call spend ceiling, enforced by the `claude` CLI itself (--max-budget-usd, provider
# built-in -- not a threshold this kit computes or compares). A backstop for one genuinely
# wedged call, not a budget policy: self-tuning across members happens via the fleet's own
# spend visibility (scripts/fleet_db.py spend) and agents/ceo.md, not this number.
FLEET_MAX_BUDGET_USD=5

# Model/limits for the builder (worktree_builder.sh). Same reasoning as the reviewer above --
# bump the model for a repo where correctness stakes are high, tune turns/timeout by how big
# a typical backlog item actually is for your codebase.
FLEET_BUILDER_MODEL=sonnet
FLEET_BUILDER_MAX_TURNS=60
FLEET_BUILDER_TIMEOUT=2400

# --- gru orchestration -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# gru is the orchestrator (see docs/gru-minions.md) -- it chooses WHICH items to build and
# whether a tier is worth spending on at all. HOW MANY is arithmetic, and arithmetic lives in
# scripts/fanout.py, not in a model's prose: 69 real fanouts of a model deciding N by judgment
# produced N wandering 1-4 with no pattern, because each `claude -p` pass re-derives it from
# scratch and cannot see its own history.
#
# gru packs each hour with work sized to what maxx says the hour affords, in PERCENT OF WEEK
# -- never dollars (this fleet runs on a subscription; dollars are not the constraint). maxx
# applies both buffers itself (weekly_max 0.925, per_diem_use 0.95), so `per_diem_hourly_pct`
# is already safe to spend to. gru takes 70% of it; the other eight members spend the rest.
# Nothing to configure here -- the numbers are live, and a hardcoded budget was exactly the
# magic number this replaced.
#
# Real usage/pacing signal (optional) — scripts/maxx_reader.py, verified live 2026-08-22
# against api.meetmaxx.co's MCP endpoint. When all three are set, gru reads this directly as
# part of its own runway calculation. Leave unset (most consumers will) to skip this entirely
# — gru falls back to a small fixed N per its own charter's escalation rule.
# FLEET_MAXX_URL=https://api.meetmaxx.co
# FLEET_MAXX_HANDLE=your_handle
# FLEET_MAXX_KEY=your_key

# --- logging ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where scheduler scripts write their logs. Each script appends its own filename under this
# directory (e.g. $FLEET_LOG_DIR/codereview.log). Every pass ALSO appends a structured record to
# $FLEET_LOG_DIR/runs.jsonl (run_report.py's contract) -- that file is what fleet_view_server.py
# tails.
FLEET_LOG_DIR=~/Library/Logs/fleet-kit

# --- live view (optional) ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# scripts/fleet_view_server.py: a live window (SSE-fed page) onto runs.jsonl + `gh pr/issue
# list`. No DB. GET routes are unauthenticated by design (it's a read-only status page); every
# WRITE route (run_now, fleet_toggle, steer, prune, close_pr, create_issue, comment_issue,
# close_issue) requires FLEET_API_KEY below. See schedulers/README.md's `view` row.
FLEET_VIEW_PORT=8420
FLEET_VIEW_GH_POLL_S=20

# Shared secret for this server's WRITE routes, sent as the `X-Fleet-Key` header.
#
# Set this if the view is reachable from anywhere but the fleet box itself. Requests from
# localhost skip the check (so an operator on the box is never locked out); everything else
# must present the key. If this is UNSET, remote writes are refused outright rather than
# allowed -- fail closed, because an unset key silently meaning "no authentication" is exactly
# how /api/run_now ended up world-callable through a Cloudflare tunnel (found live 2026-08-25:
# any stranger could POST a member name and spawn `claude -p --dangerously-skip-permissions`
# on the box, spending the account pool's budget with repo write access and a gh token).
#
# "It's behind a tunnel" is NOT authentication -- a tunnel publishes the service, it does not
# guard it. Generate one with:  python3 -c "import secrets;print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"
FLEET_API_KEY=

# --- outage pagers (README step 6) --------------------------------------------------------------
# account_health_check.sh / tunnel_health_check.sh / path_health_check.sh are the fleet's ONLY
# outage pagers. entrypoint.sh's container-native cron lines re-source THIS file immediately
# before each tick (gh#279), so a value edited here is picked up on the very next tick -- no
# restart needed.
#
# Unlike every optional credential above, these three are NOT "leave unset to skip that lane" --
# each script hard-requires every var it reads via a `:?` guard, so an unset value here does not
# disable the pager, it crashes it every single tick, forever, silently (gh#301: confirmed live,
# 96+ identical crashes across days on one instance, zero .{account,tunnel,path}_health_paged
# .state files ever created -- the exact "looks busy, pages nobody" failure mode this kit's
# README warns about). There is no safe default for any of the three; leaving this section
# unset is a choice to run with zero outage visibility, not a no-op.
#
# NTFY_TOPIC: the ntfy.sh topic to page (a POST to https://ntfy.sh/<topic> -- anyone subscribed
# to that exact string gets the push). No default exists anywhere in this kit; pick a topic
# nobody else can guess -- ntfy.sh topics are unauthenticated, so whoever knows the string can
# both read and post to it.
NTFY_TOPIC=
# PUBLIC_URL: this instance's public tunnel root, e.g. https://your-tunnel-host.example.com/.
# tunnel_health_check.sh curls this for an HTTP 200 and, on failure, self-heals by rewriting
# Cloudflare ingress for its hostname -- a WRONG value here makes that rewrite target the wrong
# hostname, which is worse than the pager just crashing. Confirm the real value (e.g. `curl -s
# -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' "$PUBLIC_URL"` -> 200) before setting it.
PUBLIC_URL=
# PUBLIC_PATH_URL: this instance's real Caddy-fronted dashboard path, e.g.
# https://your-tunnel-host.example.com/fleet/<instance-name> -- PUBLIC_URL's root alone falls
# through Caddy's default route and never touches this. Same no-safe-default caution as
# PUBLIC_URL above; confirm with a direct curl before setting.
PUBLIC_PATH_URL=

# --- deploy driver (optional) -----------------------------------------------------------------
# See scripts/deploy_driver.md for the 3-function contract. Leave blank to skip deploy
# entirely — the fleet still builds/reviews/merges without ever calling a deployer.
FLEET_DEPLOY_DRIVER=

# --- messenger driver (optional) ----------------------------------------------------------------
# See scripts/messenger_driver.md for the 3-function contract. Leave blank and
# dont-shoot-the-messenger logs "local-only" and no-ops -- a valid mode, not an error.
# scripts/messenger_driver_local.sh ships with the kit: it's the right driver whenever your
# dashboard (fleet_view_server.py) already reads FLEET_LOG_DIR straight off the same disk --
# ship_logs is then a no-op-but-verified (confirms the dir is actually there/writable), and
# relay_transcript reads the same per-member .log the dashboard's own "view transcript" does.
# Point this at your own driver instead if logs/transcripts need to leave this box.
FLEET_MESSENGER_DRIVER=/fleet-kit/scripts/messenger_driver_local.sh

# Your driver script needs its OWN credentials to actually reach your host/platform (an SSH
# deploy key, a DigitalOcean/Fly/Render API token, a kubeconfig — whatever your `deploy`
# function shells out to). Same rule as Claude credentials above: never bake these into the
# image. Two options, same pattern the kit already uses:
#   - env var at `podman run` time:    -e DEPLOY_SSH_KEY_PATH=/run/secrets/deploy_key
#   - bind-mount the secret itself:    -v ~/.ssh/deploy_key:/run/secrets/deploy_key:ro
# Read them inside your own deploy_driver.sh — this kit never touches them directly.

# --- scheduler cadences (seconds, for systemd timer / launchd StartInterval templates) --------
FLEET_CADENCE_GITPULL=300
FLEET_CADENCE_RANK=3600
FLEET_CADENCE_BUILD=3600
FLEET_CADENCE_REVIEW=900
FLEET_CADENCE_CEO=3600
# architect and ceo are DAILY by design (see agents/architect.md's "cadence is the hidden
# defect" note) — expressed as a calendar time, not an interval; see the plist template.
FLEET_ARCHITECT_HOUR_UTC=15
FLEET_ARCHITECT_MINUTE_UTC=10
